for years the tech industry has been  claiming that robots will be delivering  packages any day now delivery service  using autonomous drones to deliver goods  with flying drones taking a nice robot  for a walk and it's been nearly 10 years  since jeff bezos announced amazon drone  delivery new half hour delivery half  hour delivery half hour delivery well  since that bezos clip he's grown his net  worth from a measly 25 billion to over  200 billion and we still don't have  drones delivering our packages so what  gives jeff  he clearly has the money to do it so why  hasn't it happened is robotic delivery  pure hype or could we actually see this  take off in the next few years to get to  the bottom of this i dug into dozens of  different projects that are working on  this problem and found some really  strange things  let's start with the amazon drone  delivery program and then go into the  more advanced projects after that  although there were other companies  talking about using drones to deliver  small packages before bezos made his  announcement it was the amazon program  that really brought mainstream attention  to the idea dji had just launched the  phantom quadcopter and for the first  time drones were no longer just for  nerds who were willing to solder  different parts together tons of people  were getting drones for christmas and  they were remarkably easy to use every  few months a new drone would come out  with more capabilities and usually it  would be cheaper too using drones to  deliver packages seemed like the next  logical step so in 2013 amazon launched  their prime air division which was  focused on developing all of the  necessary technology to enable this new  delivery method dji drones made for  great toys and could take some pretty  solid video as well but there was no way  that amazon was going to build its new  drone delivery business on top of a  consumer drone platform instead amazon  would need to figure out how to build a  new delivery drone that could fly  completely autonomously then make sure  it didn't break down in bad weather and  finally figure out how to manufacture  millions of them it wouldn't be easy but  bezos had billions of dollars at his  disposal and clearly believed that this  was the future overnight there were tons  of questions about the program was it  safe to have heavy packages flying  around the sky would these drones  contribute to noise pollution and would  this interfere with planes or  helicopters these are reasonable  questions and in order to answer them  amazon would have to deal with the  united states federal aviation  administration or faa see the faa had  already been dealing with a number of  incidents involving drones before bezos  announced his plans to launch millions  more drones had gotten so cheap that  random people could basically fly them  anywhere they wanted to so the faa  cracked down and mandated that drones  fly no higher than 400 feet no faster  than 100 miles per hour and remain  within the pilot's line of sight  additionally these new drone delivery  programs would have to submit  applications to the faa before they  began service to prove that they were  safe amazon's a huge company though so  these regulations weren't too much of an  issue and bezos was able to complete the  first drone delivery to an actual  customer in 2016. it was another big  moment in the press but since then there  hasn't been much development and just a  few months ago wired reported that the  project was winding down after failing  to gain traction big companies fail to  deliver on new technologies all the time  but that doesn't mean that we won't see  drone delivery become commonplace soon  there are plenty of other entrepreneurs  working on the problem and everyone is  taking a slightly different approach  amazon was in a tough situation because  they had to consider every possible  delivery location some people live in  houses surrounded by trees some  neighborhoods have lots of power lines  to worry about designing a  one-size-fits-all solution is incredibly  difficult so most smaller companies have  tried to find workarounds there's a  startup named flirty that's been working  on drone delivery since 2013 and they  use a retractable line to drop the  package to the ground this keeps the  drone safe from obstacles and allows  them to land in different locations it's  a bit of a weird idea but in some areas  it could make a lot of sense flirty's  drone delivery service still isn't  widely available but they've run some  food delivery tests and are continuing  to work with the us government to get  regulatory approval another startup  named matternet actually sets up  dedicated takeoff and landing stations  everywhere that they operate now this is  obviously a bit of a restriction on the  service since it means that the drones  can only deliver between specific  locations but matternet is focused on  medical deliveries so they really only  need to set up one station at each  hospital to enable their service it's  pretty far from the original vision of  ubiquitous drone delivery but if  matternet can solve the core problem of  point-to-point drone delivery between  hospitals they could potentially expand  services from there and medical  deliveries are a really interesting use  case for drones zipline is one of the  most successful startups in this area  and they got their start by delivering  blood donations in rwanda people tend to  think that america is always the best  place to launch a new technology but  that couldn't be further from the truth  growing a drone delivery company is  actually easier in rwanda than america  for a few reasons first demand for this  service is incredibly high moving blood  from central donation centers out to  rural hospitals is incredibly difficult  through normal means in rwanda there  just isn't enough blood to store extra  donations at every single hospital in  the country and driving blood around  just isn't feasible in a life or death  situation  where every second matters zipline's  drone deliveries save lives and that's  led to the rwandan government actually  supporting the initiative and speeding  up regulatory approvals building a new  technology like drone delivery requires  honing in on the highest value use case  and building out from there zipline is  now expanding to other markets but by  starting with blood delivery they were  able to test out the basic technology  before scaling it so it seems like we're  pretty close to getting life-saving  medical supplies delivered by drone in  the near future but what if i just want  a pizza delivered quickly and cheaply  well college students eat a lot of pizza  and there's a different kind of drone  delivery company that's having a lot of  success on college campuses instead of  dealing with the complexities of flying  a quadrotor drone around starship  robotics has focused on building robots  that just drive on the sidewalk  obviously there are some pros and cons  to this approach first off you can drive  these robots basically anywhere that a  human can walk this is great for college  campuses that are designed with  pedestrians in mind most of the  sidewalks are paved and handicapped  accessible although these starship  robots can hop a curb if they need to  more importantly on a college campus  there is a very high density of  potential customers thousands of  students are ready to use the service  and are already familiar with ordering  things online it's also easy to create a  high resolution 3d map of the entire  campus so the robots know exactly where  to go it's not a completely controlled  environment but it's a bit more  forgiving than busy city streets  sticking to small campuses also means  that it's easier to keep the robots from  getting vandalized there's a long  history of tech companies putting things  on the sidewalk and just hoping that  everyone will behave but that's usually  not what happens if starship and the  other sidewalk robot companies want to  keep their fleets from getting  absolutely destroyed they'll need to  focus on deploying them in areas that  already have tight security like college  campuses the list of drone delivery  robots gets a lot crazier from here but  first we need to take a moment to talk  about the human impact of automation  whenever tech companies announce new  robots everyone assumes that millions of  people are about to lose their jobs now  that certainly hasn't been the case  during previous waves of automation but  it's still worth discussing when the  automobile started replacing the horse  and carriage people started asking  questions what would happen to all the  workers who tended to the horses and  drove the carriages would we see a  massive wave of unemployment as soon as  everyone started driving themselves that  obviously didn't happen  cars took over slowly and new jobs were  created in the process  and something similar is happening with  these sidewalk robots see these college  kids wouldn't be paying for human  courier services if these robots didn't  exist they would just go pick up their  items directly this isn't a situation  where a human delivery driver is losing  a job the only person who's losing a job  is the college student who no longer has  to go pick something up and these robots  aren't even fully autonomous yet each  fleet has remote drivers that can step  in and control the units from a computer  if something goes wrong so instead of  putting people out of work this actually  creates new jobs and there's a different  sidewalk robot company named coco that  actually uses remote drivers exclusively  now i'm sure they're planning to layer  in self-driving technology down the road  but for now every robot in their fleet  is controlled by a human each sidewalk  robot company is approaching the problem  differently which is really valuable  when no one knows exactly what will work  best amazon was originally aiming for a  universal solution that could work  everywhere but it looks like a segmented  approach might be better a hospital  network will have different needs from a  college campus and this will lead to  radically different robotic solutions  and amazon is actually working on their  own sidewalk robots now so it seems like  they might wind up offering drone  delivery after all even if these drones  won't fly but all of these sidewalk  robots have one big problem they're very  slow that's great from a safety  perspective but what if you just have to  have food from a particular restaurant  that's all the way across town waiting  two hours as your delivery robot crawls  across the city at five miles per hour  doesn't exactly sound like innovation to  me well there are a few approaches that  could speed things up the most ambitious  project i found is actually inspired by  elon musk's tunneling project pipedream  labs is a startup that plans to build  delivery robots that will travel through  underground pipes this solves a few  problems while introducing a bunch of  new ones obviously with an underground  pipe you don't need to worry about bad  weather vandalism or pedestrian safety  you do however have to worry about  actually getting the pipes in the ground  the boring company obviously ran into a  lot of issues while trying to dig  massive tunnels throughout cities there  are tons of safety concerns when you're  about to drive an enormous drill right  underneath people's houses getting  governments on board with these types of  projects is incredibly difficult but  pipedream has a solution they're  planning on using standard 12-inch pvc  pipe which is already commonly used on  construction sites city workers know how  to install and maintain this type of  pipe so it's a lot easier to get  approved and instead of paying for a  massive drill to build a huge tunnel  pipe dream just needs to convince cities  to install empty pvc pipes next to the  existing water main lines once the pipes  are installed autonomous drones can  drive through these tunnels carrying  packages to their destinations because  the pipes are completely self-contained  the pods can travel 15 times faster than  a sidewalk robot it's not going to be  right for every kind of delivery but it  could be a useful option for certain  types of goods obviously tunnel based  approaches to robotic delivery take a  long time to roll out so this particular  technology is unlikely to be widely  available anytime soon but there is  still one other major option when it  comes to drone delivery and i honestly  think that this one might be right  around the corner all delivery companies  have to optimize around two factors  route density which is the number of  drop-offs you can make on a delivery  route and drop size which is the number  of parcels you can drop off at each stop  if you can make lots of deliveries in  one particular area the cost per  delivery will be low similarly if you  can drop off a bunch of packages at a  single location your cost per package  will be low the problem with most drone  delivery programs is that the drones are  designed to carry just one package and  after it drops it off the drone has to  return to a home base to recharge this  drives up the cost of delivery and  pretty soon you're better off just using  a delivery truck after all the average  ups truck makes 120 stops per day to  deliver up to a thousand individual  packages that's really hard to compete  with and that's why self-driving car  companies are starting to roll out  package delivery services cruise  automation is one of the largest  autonomous vehicle companies and  although they are primarily focused on  building a fleet of robo taxis they  recently showcased a fully autonomous  electric vehicle that can be outfitted  specifically for deliveries it might not  feel as futuristic as having a humanoid  robot knock on your door but  self-driving cars do solve a remarkable  amount of the problems faced by the  other drone delivery solutions they're  fast they work in any weather and they  can handle heavy packages but most  importantly they can optimize for route  density and drop size just like a  traditional delivery truck so back to  the original question when is drone  delivery actually going to be a reality  well if we're still using the original  amazon vision as our goal it could be a  while i think we need to update our  expectations a bit any technology that  improves the speed and efficiency of  deliveries is a win in my book and most  likely we're going to wind up with a  blend of technologies super valuable  deliveries like blood and medical  supplies will arrive through the air  while heavier stuff will continue to be  delivered on wheels we're clearly still  in the early days of robotics generally  but i think we could start seeing some  really incredible breakthroughs over the  next decade but let me know when you  think robotic delivery will actually  arrive and please check out this video  about the teslabot next it covers a  bunch of cool ai stuff that elon musk is  working on and i think you'll really  like it

